---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FailureCase from '../../components/FailureCase.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Failure Museum" lang="en">
  <div class="page-header container">
    <h1>Failure Museum</h1>
    <p class="page-desc">Cases where simulations diverged, results were clearly wrong, or mistakes were made. The focus isn't "I was wrong" but "why I made this mistake" and "how to avoid it next time."</p>
    <blockquote class="museum-quote">
      Beginners don't need success stories — they need the safety to fail. When you can clearly articulate your mistakes, you build not just professional authority but personal trust.
    </blockquote>
  </div>

  <div class="failure-list container-wide">
    <FailureCase
      title="My First Contact Analysis: Forgot to Set Friction Coefficient"
      date="2025-09-15"
      software="ANSYS"
      physics="Contact"
      whatHappened="Running a bolted joint contact analysis. After 4 hours it finally converged. But the results showed the bolt and flange penetrating each other — contact surfaces passed right through one another. Displacement results were completely unreliable."
      whyWrong="I thought selecting 'Frictional' contact type was enough. I didn't know I had to manually set the friction coefficient. The default was 0 (frictionless), meaning no tangential resistance — surfaces could slide into penetration. I also didn't check the Contact Status plot, only looked at displacement contours and assumed convergence meant correctness."
      whatLearned="① After contact analysis, the FIRST thing to check is Contact Status, not displacement. ② Friction coefficient must match actual conditions (steel-steel typically 0.1-0.2). ③ Add Contact Tool to check penetration — penetration > 5% of element size is unacceptable."
      lang="en"
    />

    <FailureCase
      title="Coarse Mesh Gave False Stress Concentration"
      date="2025-11-08"
      software="Abaqus"
      physics="Structural Statics"
      whatHappened="Analyzing a tension plate with a circular hole using default mesh. Stress distribution looked reasonable with peak at hole edge. But compared to the Kirsch analytical solution, the error was 35%."
      whyWrong="Classic beginner mistake: ran one mesh and published results without convergence study. Default mesh had only 2-3 elements around the hole, far too few to capture the stress gradient. I also confused 'converged solver' with 'accurate results' — solver convergence ≠ result accuracy."
      whatLearned="① Every analysis needs a mesh convergence study (at least 3 mesh densities, track key quantities). ② Local refinement at stress concentrations (holes, fillets, load application points). ③ Always validate against analytical solutions when available. ④ 'Converged' only means iteration tolerance is met — not that results are physically accurate."
      lang="en"
    />

    <FailureCase
      title="Density conversion in a consistent mm–N–s unit system"
      date="2026-10-23"
      reviewExample={true}
      software="ANSYS"
      physics="Structural Dynamics"
      whatHappened="The previous account mentioned an anomalous modal frequency, but supplied no model, input file, solver log or results. Its frequency, debugging duration and event date remain unverified. This entry records a source-based correction; no simulation was run."
      whyWrong="MPa and mm are compatible. In a consistent mm–N–s system, mass is measured in Mg (tonne) and density in Mg/mm³. An assumed 7850 kg/m³ converts to 7.85×10⁻⁹ Mg/mm³. Entering 7850 directly would inflate density by 10¹². Ansys 2025 R2 lists the MPA system as mm, Mg, s, °C. /UNITS only labels the database and does not convert its data."
      whatLearned="Document all length, mass, time, force, stress and density units and check their consistency. Assess frequencies against geometry, materials, boundary conditions and reference solutions. A fixed frequency range is not a universal acceptance rule. Actual inputs, logs and results are still needed to verify this case."
      lang="en"
    ><p>Sources reviewed: <a href="https://ansyshelp.ansys.com/public/views/secured/corp/v252/en/ans_cmd/Hlp_C_UNITS.html">Ansys 2025 R2 /UNITS</a> · <a href="https://ansyshelp.ansys.com/public/views/secured/corp/v252/en/ans_cou/Hlp_G_COU1_3.html">System of Units</a>. Only the cited sections were checked; the example was not run.</p></FailureCase>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-header { margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl); }
  .page-header h1 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 2rem; }
  .page-desc { color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-top: var(--space-sm); max-width: 640px; }
  .museum-quote { margin-top: var(--space-lg); font-size: 0.95rem; border-left-color: #f59e0b; background: #fffbeb; }
  .failure-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-xl); max-width: 860px; margin: 0 auto; }
</style>
